                    insight_excellent:      `Excellent. The hybrid posture is the right answer: 12(b)(6) on Count Three, targeted EVPI discovery on the LP letter and the side-letter thread, and a sealed settlement on Counts One and Two below Mantorra reservation price. Doctrine sharp, decision tree priced both sides, principals translated correctly.`,
                    insight_good:           `Good. The strategy is defensible across doctrine and economics, but at least one principal-translation move or one EVPI lever was suboptimal. Re-run with the Branch Beta two-tree comparison and the Branch Gamma elevator-pitch translation drills.`,
                    insight_developing:     `Developing. The posture is coherent on one or two dimensions but not yet integrated. The most common failure mode at this score band is pricing settlement before completing the case-theory matrix, or quoting Twombly in the room with a non-lawyer principal.`,
                    insight_beginner:       `Beginner. The trace shows the Branch Alpha failure mode — pricing the case before reading it. Restart with the doctrinal-anchor pre-reads (Twombly/Iqbal, Restatement §552, Korea Supply) and the Mnookin–Kornhauser bargaining-in-the-shadow-of-the-law framing before the next attempt.`,
                    brief_tag_p1:           `Round 1 · Decision 1 of 4`,
                    brief_title_p1:         `Open the partner meeting`,
                    brief_text_p1:          `It is 09:00 Tuesday. The CEO 23:14 email is on your screen — settle at USD 3.5M by Friday. Reisinger-Voss is on the Halvorsen Drake line. Krishnamurthy is on video from her home office. Wexler-Marín stated method: we do not price a case before we read it. How do you open?`,
                    decision_label_p1:      `Opening posture for the partner meeting`,
                    p1_opt_a:               `Accept the CEO directive — settle at USD 3.5M before reading the case`,
                    p1_opt_b:               `Open with a three-count case-theory matrix before pricing anything`,
                    p1_opt_c:               `Order Reisinger-Voss to draft 12(b)(6) on all three counts now`,
                    p1_opt_d:               `Authorise full discovery roadmap before any motion or settlement`,
                    p1_fb_a:                `Pricing before reading is the Branch Alpha failure mode. The CEO number is below the plausible Mantorra reservation price; you are likely buying a no-resolution outcome plus signaling weakness to other dissatisfied customers.`,
                    p1_fb_b:                `Correct opening. Doctrine before economics. The 3×5 matrix forces the team to rank counts by seriousness, locate the dispositive factual disputes, and identify which discovery would move the tree most — before any settlement number is debated.`,
                    p1_fb_c:                `Scattershot 12(b)(6) is sloppy motion craft. NDCA judges read scattershot motions as desperation; the surgical 12(b)(6) on Count Three is the better filing because Count Three is genuinely weak and the others are not.`,
                    p1_fb_d:                `Full discovery is over-spend before doctrine is clean. FRCP 26(b)(1) proportionality cuts against you — a USD 38M demand with a USD 4.8M LoL cap is proportionality-relevant and the cap shrinks the discovery footprint.`,
                    brief_tag_p2:           `Round 1 · Decision 2 of 4`,
                    brief_title_p2:         `Count One — Breach of contract`,
                    brief_text_p2:          `Mantorra alleges the late SOC 2 Type II caused a USD 21M LP-commitment loss. The MSA limitation-of-liability clause caps direct damages at twelve months of fees (~USD 4.8M) and excludes consequentials. The November 2024 side letter extends "audit deliverables generally" by up to forty-five days for "good-faith auditor delays" — interpretive ambiguity. The LP rejection letter, listing the LP actual reasons, is in plaintiff custody. How do you frame the defense?`,
                    decision_label_p2:      `Defense frame for Count One`,
                    p2_opt_a:               `Concede liability on Count One and focus on quantum negotiation`,
                    p2_opt_b:               `Lead with the LoL cap (USD 4.8M) — direct vs consequential damages frame`,
                    p2_opt_c:               `Argue the November 2024 side-letter forty-five-day extension covers the Type II`,
                    p2_opt_d:               `Stack: side-letter defense + LoL cap + attack causation via the LP rejection letter`,
                    p2_fb_a:                `Premature concession. The LoL cap and the side-letter ambiguity together convert this from a USD 21M exposure to a USD 4.8M exposure with a real defense on causation. Conceding gives away the cap argument.`,
                    p2_fb_b:                `Solid frame. The cap argument is the load-bearing move — it shrinks Count One exposure from USD 21M to USD 4.8M if it survives. But you have left the side-letter defense and the causation attack on the table.`,
                    p2_fb_c:                `The side-letter defense is real but interpretive — Reisinger-Voss will press whether it covers the Type II or only Type I and component reports. Used alone it is fragile; layered with the cap and causation, it is robust.`,
                    p2_fb_d:                `Strongest frame. You have stacked three defenses: the LoL cap shrinks the headline number, the side-letter creates a contract-interpretation fight, and the LP rejection letter (once obtained via targeted discovery) is the EVPI-positive document that may collapse causation entirely.`,
                    brief_tag_p3:           `Round 1 · Decision 3 of 4`,
                    brief_title_p3:         `Count Two — Negligent misrepresentation`,
                    brief_text_p3:          `Restatement (Second) §552 was adopted by CA courts in commercial contexts via Bily v. Arthur Young. Salazar 14:22 PT recorded statement on December 14, 2025: SOC 2 was "complete and audited." Auditor evidence-gathering ended December 19; Type I issued January 11. Mantorra CTO held Type I documentation and an auditor email thread acknowledging the Type II evidence-gathering window. Where do you attack?`,
                    decision_label_p3:      `Defense angle on §552`,
                    p3_opt_a:               `Deny falsity — Salazar statement was a reasonable approximation of imminent completion`,
                    p3_opt_b:               `Attack justifiable reliance — Mantorra CTO held Type I docs and the auditor email thread`,
                    p3_opt_c:               `Combined frame: dispute falsity at the moment AND attack justifiable reliance`,
                    p3_opt_d:               `Concede §552 and focus on the pecuniary-loss measure`,
                    p3_fb_a:                `Falsity-only is the weaker single-element attack. A jury can call "complete and audited" false when the auditor was still gathering evidence. The reliance attack is doctrinally sharper because it uses Mantorra own evidence against them.`,
                    p3_fb_b:                `Doctrinally precise. Where the plaintiff is a sophisticated commercial actor with parallel evidence of the underlying fact, attacking justifiable reliance is the highest-yield defensive move under §552. Bily reliance-gap doctrine does the work.`,
                    p3_fb_c:                `Strongest. Combined frame forces Mantorra to win both elements. Falsity is a jury question; reliance is more legal-friendly given the documentary record. Together they make Count Two summary-judgment-viable rather than trial-only.`,
                    p3_fb_d:                `Premature concession again. §552 is doctrinally interesting but defensible — the reliance gap is the most powerful move and you have just abandoned it.`,
                    brief_tag_p4:           `Round 1 · Decision 4 of 4`,
                    brief_title_p4:         `Count Three — Tortious interference`,
                    brief_text_p4:          `California tortious interference with prospective economic advantage requires under Korea Supply Co. v. Lockheed Martin (29 Cal. 4th 1134, 2003) an independently wrongful act — tortious, criminal, or statutory/regulatory violation. The complaint alleges a Plumeline.io customer-success person at a February vendor dinner described Mantorra to two other LPs as "a difficult customer with unrealistic compliance demands." Twombly/Iqbal plausibility applies. What is the play?`,
                    decision_label_p4:      `Strategy on Count Three`,
                    p4_opt_a:               `File a surgical 12(b)(6) on Count Three under Korea Supply and Twombly/Iqbal`,
                    p4_opt_b:               `Pursue full discovery on the vendor-dinner episode before any motion`,
                    p4_opt_c:               `Concede Count Three and offer a corrective statement to the LP base`,
                    p4_opt_d:               `Bury Count Three inside a scattershot 12(b)(6) on all three counts`,
                    p4_fb_a:                `Right move. Korea Supply requires an independently wrongful act; generic disparagement does not clear the bar. Twombly/Iqbal plausibility kills the conclusory causation chain to two non-party LP decisions. Reisinger-Voss prior NDCA 12(b)(6) wins suggest a 35% grant rate — high enough to be EV-positive at USD 280K spend.`,
                    p4_fb_b:                `Discovery on the dinner episode is exactly what Mantorra wants — it converts the weakest count into a fact-intensive sideshow that consumes legal budget without changing exposure on Counts One or Two.`,
                    p4_fb_c:                `Conceding Count Three signals that the company believes the disparagement happened as alleged. A corrective statement to the LP base is the worst possible Series D narrative — it creates a public footprint where none existed.`,
                    p4_fb_d:                `Scattershot 12(b)(6) reduces the per-count grant probability across the board. Surgical filings on doctrinally weak counts are the high-yield move; widening the motion dilutes the signal to the court.`,
                    brief_tag_p5:           `Round 2 · Decision 1 of 2`,
                    brief_title_p5:         `Pre-trial posture — the five-branch decision tree`,
                    brief_text_p5:          `Mnookin–Kornhauser frames pre-trial bargaining as a function of the joint distribution of expected trial outcomes and litigation-cost trajectories. Branch A (settle now USD 3.5M) is below Mantorra estimated reservation price (USD 4–7M sealed). Branch E (trial-track) carries a median verdict of USD 12M plus USD 4.2M fees plus reputational discount. Where does the EV-and-variance math point?`,
                    decision_label_p5:      `Pre-trial posture`,
                    p5_opt_a:               `Branch A — Settle now at USD 3.5M (CEO preference)`,
                    p5_opt_b:               `Branch B — Limited fact discovery, then settle once Count One strength is empirical`,
                    p5_opt_c:               `Branch C — Narrow 12(b)(6) on Count Three only, hold the rest`,
                    p5_opt_d:               `Branch D — Push to summary judgment on all three counts`,
                    p5_opt_e:               `Branch E — Trial-track to verdict`,
                    p5_opt_f:               `Hybrid — 12(b)(6) on Count Three + targeted discovery + sealed settlement on Counts One/Two`,
                    p5_fb_a:                `USD 3.5M is below Mantorra plausible reservation price. The expected outcome is rejection plus USD 200K of negotiation cost, after which you settle higher anyway. The CEO preference is real but the number is wrong.`,
                    p5_fb_b:                `Branch B is reasonable but loses the optionality of the Count Three motion. EV midpoint USD 4.3M; you have left the cheapest doctrinal win on the table.`,
                    p5_fb_c:                `Branch C is half the right answer — the Count Three motion is correct, but without targeted discovery on the LP letter and side-letter thread, you cannot price the sealed-settlement floor on Counts One and Two.`,
                    p5_fb_d:                `SJ on all three counts is over-spend. SJ on Count Two is the realistic win; SJ on Count One faces the causation fact-fight head-on. USD 1.4M for a partial win is worse than USD 2.1M for a hybrid that closes the matter.`,
                    p5_fb_e:                `Trial-track produces a long right tail (15% probability of USD 18–38M) and a public verdict regardless of outcome. A public verdict at any value is materially worse than a sealed settlement below USD 8M in the Series D diligence window.`,
                    p5_fb_f:                `Strongest. Hybrid expected total ~USD 2.1M, well below the CEO USD 3.5M and below Mantorra reservation price. Wins on doctrine, on Mnookin–Kornhauser ZOPA economics, and on the diligence-narrative dimension that decides Series D risk surface.`,
                    brief_tag_p6:           `Round 2 · Decision 2 of 2`,
                    brief_title_p6:         `EVPI — what discovery moves the tree most?`,
                    brief_text_p6:          `Two documents dominate the expected-value-of-perfect-information calculus. First, the LP rejection letter — if it lists six reasons before SOC 2 timing, Count One causation collapses and Plumeline.io reservation price drops by USD 3M+. Second, the side-letter interpretive email thread — if it establishes the parties understood the side letter to cover the Type II, the breach claim collapses. EVPI on the pair is USD 1.2–2.4M; targeted-discovery cost is roughly USD 80K. What do you authorise?`,
                    decision_label_p6:      `Discovery scope`,
                    p6_opt_a:               `Targeted: subpoena the LP rejection letter + the side-letter interpretive email thread`,
                    p6_opt_b:               `Broad: depose every witness, request all custodial files`,
                    p6_opt_c:               `No discovery — settle now to avoid spend`,
                    p6_opt_d:               `Symmetric: depose Salazar and the Mantorra CTO only — no documents`,
                    p6_fb_a:                `Right move. EVPI ~USD 1.2–2.4M against ~USD 80K cost is overwhelmingly accretive. The LP letter and the side-letter thread are the two pieces of information that compress the tree most. FRCP 26(b)(1) proportionality argument supports the narrow scope.`,
                    p6_fb_b:                `Broad discovery violates FRCP 26(b)(1) proportionality and burns USD 1.8M of budget without compressing the tree. Mantorra plaintiff-side counsel will welcome it — every deposition is leverage on a contingency budget.`,
                    p6_fb_c:                `No discovery means you settle without the LP letter — pricing the case at peak uncertainty. The settlement floor is USD 3M+ higher than it would be with the EVPI-positive documents in hand.`,
                    p6_fb_d:                `Depositions without the underlying documents are weak — a deponent will not characterise documents you cannot impeach with. The two documents drive the EVPI; the depositions are downstream.`,
                    brief_tag_p7:           `Round 3 · Decision 1 of 2`,
                    brief_title_p7:         `The CEO conversation`,
                    brief_text_p7:          `The CEO wants the matter gone at USD 3.5M by Friday. The hybrid expected cost is USD 2.1M, takes four to six months, exposes Plumeline.io to interim headline risk during the eleven-week Series D diligence kickoff window. CEO objects on cost, time, distraction. Branch Gamma reminds you: the CEO does not need to know what Twombly is; the CEO needs the answer in business English. How do you handle this conversation?`,
                    decision_label_p7:      `CEO conversation move`,
                    p7_opt_a:               `Tell the CEO this is a legal matter and ask him to trust your judgment`,
                    p7_opt_b:               `Walk the CEO through Twombly plausibility and the EVPI math line by line`,
                    p7_opt_c:               `Reframe via Mantorra reservation-price logic and the Series D diligence narrative`,
                    p7_opt_d:               `Capitulate — agree to settle at USD 3.5M as the CEO requested`,
                    p7_fb_a:                `"Trust me" is the worst answer to a CEO who has been awake at 23:14 worrying about a USD 38M complaint. It tells him you cannot translate, which is the principal-agent failure mode that defines weak GCs.`,
                    p7_fb_b:                `Right doctrine, wrong audience. Twombly to a non-lawyer CEO is the Branch Gamma failure mode — accurate but unparseable. The CEO needs the answer in business English, not the doctrinal bibliography.`,
                    p7_fb_c:                `Strongest. The CEO USD 3.5M is below Mantorra reservation price, so the real comparison is USD 3.5M-rejected-then-USD-5M-settled vs USD 2.1M-hybrid. A leaked sealed settlement during diligence is a worse signal than a routine motion-practice diligence narrative. The CEO can hear those two arguments.`,
                    p7_fb_d:                `Capitulating destroys the GC-as-operator credibility you have spent the morning building. Worse, the CEO number is a likely no-resolution outcome and you have just promised it would resolve the matter.`,
                    brief_tag_p8:           `Round 3 · Decision 2 of 2`,
                    brief_title_p8:         `The Audit Committee — reserve and disclosure posture`,
                    brief_text_p8:          `The Audit Committee chair is a former Big-Four CFO. Her concerns are reputational and disclosure: materiality, reputational discount, reserve. Realistic exposure on the hybrid path, given the LoL cap, the side-letter defense, and the §552 reliance gap, is USD 2–6M reasonably-possible. What posture do you bring to the audit committee?`,
                    decision_label_p8:      `Audit Committee posture`,
                    p8_opt_a:               `Disclose the full USD 38M demand as a material litigation contingency`,
                    p8_opt_b:               `Reserve USD 2.5M (midpoint reasonably-possible); disclosure-permitted-but-not-required`,
                    p8_opt_c:               `Reserve USD 0 — the case is defensible and disclosure is premature`,
                    p8_opt_d:               `Reserve USD 6M (top of reasonably-possible range) with full footnote disclosure`,
                    p8_fb_a:                `Disclosing the headline USD 38M crosses qualitative materiality but ignores that realistic exposure is USD 2–6M. You have just printed a number for trade press to attach to that has nothing to do with the actual exposure.`,
                    p8_fb_b:                `Strongest. Reserve at the midpoint of the reasonably-possible range, disclosure-permitted-but-not-required posture, quarterly true-up, disclosed in the management-representation letter to the auditor. Sealed-settlement optionality preserved; Series D diligence narrative intact.`,
                    p8_fb_c:                `Reserve of zero with disclosure deferred is the posture that ages worst. If the matter resolves above USD 1M the audit-committee chair will not forgive the reserve gap, and the auditor will flag it in the management letter.`,
                    p8_fb_d:                `Top-of-range reserve plus full disclosure is overreaction. Conservative, but the reputational discount during the Series D diligence window is heavy — a USD 6M booked reserve becomes the floor for any leaked-settlement narrative.`
                },
